Chapter: DiskFactsGptGuard
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog

 A partitioned disk with NO fact-store partition on it. The store has
 nowhere of its own to address, so it refuses.

 It refuses because of what writing used to do. The store's sectors 0 and 1
 are its superblock and sector 2 upward is its log; on a partitioned disk
 those are the protective MBR, the GPT header and the partition entry
 array. Measured 2026-07-27, one disk-write-fact turned a 16 MB boot image
 the firmware read as "BOOTX64.EFI ... from partition at LBA 2048" into
 "ESP at LBA 0 has an unusable BPB", and it stopped booting.

 This test is unchanged by the partition work and that is the point of
 keeping it. The store now addresses relative to a partition of its own
 type; a disk that carries a partition table and does not carry such a
 partition is still refused, and refusing it is still the only safe answer.

 The fixture is a 64 KB disk carrying the GPT signature at sector 1 and a
 sentinel byte through sector 2, which is the entry array a real GPT keeps
 there. Three readings, because a guard that only refuses is not enough --
 it has to refuse WITHOUT having already written:

   count  must stay 0. A store that counted a fact it never wrote would
          report success over a refusal.
   sig    sector 1 must still read EFI PART.
   entry  sector 2 must still read the sentinel.

 Three tests bound this behaviour between them and no two of them agree by
 accident:

   disk-facts-init      a raw disk, count must reach 1
   disk-facts-mbr-guard an MBR-only disk, count must stay 0
   facts-partition      a disk WITH a fact partition: the base is the
                        partition's LBA, the count reaches 1, and the GPT
                        header survives the write

 Any one alone would pass for a store that refused everything or for one
 that refused nothing. The last is the one that separates "safe" from
 "useful", which is the direction a guard quietly slides in.

 We say:

Section: Readings

  sig-byte-at : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  sig-byte-at (sector) (i) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector sector
    peek-byte buf i
  end

  sig-intact : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Boolean
  sig-intact (buf) (i) =
    if i >= 8 then True
    else if peek-byte buf i /= list-at [69, 70, 73, 32, 80, 65, 82, 84] i then False
    else sig-intact buf (i + 1)

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    let store = disk-load
    in let attempted = disk-write-fact store DefinitionFact "hello" 0
    in let flushed = disk-checkpoint attempted
    in act
      hdr <- block-read-sector 1
      ent <- block-read-sector 2
      print-line-uni ("count " & show (disk-fact-count attempted))
      print-line-uni ("sig " & (if sig-intact hdr 0 then "intact" else "GONE"))
      print-line-uni ("entry " & (if peek-byte ent 0 == 165 then "intact" else "GONE"))
    end
  end
